# Generated from: specs/392-recover-repaired-publication-interventions-before-implementation/requirements.md

Feature: Recover repaired publication interventions before implementation
  Bare execute may resume one stopped implementation only when existing authority
  proves the intervention was repaired by an exact canonical publication rewrite.

  @AC1 @SCN001
  Scenario: Discovery offers the existing one-time recovery tuple
    Given a failed implement run and intervention handoff bind one exact current head and incomplete owner
    And the actual owner branch differs only from the explicitly stale recorded run branch
    And the current read-only owner-bound probe returns nonempty canonical allowed paths
    And every controller handoff filename and payload issue and step derive from the current checkpoint queue and lifecycle
    When bare recovery discovery proves every repaired-publication invariant
    Then discovery returns the existing one-time recovery tuple and resume action for implement

  @AC2 @SCN002
  Scenario: Four publication labels are the complete byte change
    Given the run-head task bytes contain four detector-supported Files labels
    And the working task document is singular and Approved
    When publication repair proof projects the detector-selected rewrites
    Then only the four Files tokens become File(s) tokens
    And every other byte and line separator is identical
    And current selected publication detection converges to zero

  @AC3 @SCN003
  Scenario: Terminal workflow evidence is preserved through structure
    Given no implementation path is dirty and the only untracked files form the bounded terminal goal-ledger evidence set
    When repaired intervention discovery classifies the worktree
    Then the related regular files, identities, event sequence, shutdown reason, and byte bounds are proven
    And recovery blocks basename-only ignored files such as nested .DS_Store unless they occupy an exact documented bounded location

  @AC4 @SCN004
  Scenario: Bare run consumes and dispatches implement once
    Given discovery offers a repaired-publication recovery
    When parameter-free bare run re-proves it under the controller lease
    Then one durable repaired-publication record and one checkpoint recovery tuple are consumed
    And the failed handoff and history remain byte-identical
    And only the standard implement worker is dispatched through the controlled boundary
    And repeated discovery never offers the same recovery

  @AC5 @SCN005
  Scenario Outline: Unsafe repaired interventions stay blocked
    Given an otherwise exact repaired-publication fixture with <defect>
    When bare recovery discovery runs
    Then implementation_failed remains blocked
    And no run, recovery, handoff, task, product, or workflow-evidence bytes change

    Examples:
      | defect |
      | changed product path |
      | arbitrary spec edit |
      | unsupported publication rewrite |
      | unrelated byte mismatch |
      | staged task change |
      | wrong current head |
      | wrong issue or step |
      | wrong run or owner |
      | malformed handoff |
      | unrelated valid-looking controller handoff |
      | claimed implementation artifact |
      | missing or empty scope probe |
      | controller lock |
      | prior recovery record |
      | complete owner |
      | arbitrary untracked path |
      | partial or live goal-ledger evidence |
      | nested .DS_Store outside a documented bounded location |

  @AC6 @SCN006
  Scenario: Existing recoveries retain their behavior
    Given resumable, consumed, exhausted-loop, stale-lease, and retained-worker fixtures
    When recovery and execute suites run
    Then only the newly proven repaired-publication state gains an implement recovery
    And every existing blocker and one-time recovery remains unchanged

  @AC6 @SCN007
  Scenario: Public guidance documents the narrow recovery
    Given contributor, execute workflow, README, and changelog surfaces
    When the defect is implemented
    Then they document exact proof, under-lease revalidation, one-time consumption, preserved failure history, implement-only dispatch, and fail-closed evidence handling
    And VERSION and package version do not change
